# Chapter XV — The DOM and Events

> *"The Garden had learned to speak. It had not yet learned to listen."*

---

## 📚 What You Will Learn

In this chapter, you will learn:

- What the DOM is.
- How to select one element with `querySelector()`.
- How to select every matching element with `querySelectorAll()`.
- What a `NodeList` is.
- How to repeat an action with a `for...of` loop.
- What an event is.
- How to listen for a click with `addEventListener()`.
- How to add and remove a CSS class with `classList`.
- How these pieces combine into the Fortress navigation feature.

---

## 🌿 Why This Matters

Chapter XIV covered variables, data types, and functions.

Every example in that chapter ran the moment the page loaded, and stopped there.

None of it touched the actual page a visitor sees.

This chapter closes that gap.

The DOM is how JavaScript reaches into the real HTML sitting on the page.

Events are how JavaScript waits for a visitor to do something, instead of only running once.

Together, they are what allowed Nurgle's Garden to react to a click for the first time.

---

## 1. What Is the DOM?

The DOM stands for **Document Object Model**.

When a browser reads `index.html`, it builds a live structure in memory from that file.

That structure is the DOM.

The HTML file describes the page.

The DOM is the actual page, built from that description, and JavaScript can reach into it directly.

---

## 2. Selecting One Element with `querySelector()`

`document.querySelector()` finds the first element matching a CSS selector.

```javascript
const sanctuaryLink = document.querySelector(".navigation-link.active");
```

`.navigation-link.active` asks for an element carrying both classes at once.

The result, stored in `sanctuaryLink`, is a live reference to that real element, not a copy or a description of it.

---

## 3. Selecting Every Matching Element with `querySelectorAll()`

`document.querySelectorAll()` finds every element matching a CSS selector.

```javascript
const allLinks = document.querySelectorAll(".navigation-link");
```

This returns all six chamber links at once, not just the first.

---

## 4. What a `NodeList` Is

`querySelectorAll()` returns a `NodeList`.

A `NodeList` is a list of elements, numbered starting at `0`.

```text
NodeList(6)
0: a.navigation-link.active
1: a.navigation-link
2: a.navigation-link
3: a.navigation-link
4: a.navigation-link
5: a.navigation-link
length: 6
```

Six chambers occupy positions `0` through `5`, not `1` through `6`.

---

## 5. Counting from Zero

JavaScript, like most programming languages, counts list positions starting at `0`.

The first item sits at position `0`.

The second item sits at position `1`.

This is a fixed rule of the language, not a display choice.

---

## 6. Repeating an Action with `for...of`

A `for...of` loop visits every item in a list, one at a time.

```javascript
for (const link of allLinks) {
  console.log(link.textContent);
}
```

On each pass, JavaScript places the current item into `link`, runs the code inside the braces, then moves to the next item.

The loop stops automatically once every item has been visited.

---

## 7. Reading Text from an Element

`.textContent` reads the visible text inside an element.

```javascript
console.log(link.textContent);
```

For the Forge link, this returns the icon and label together, exactly as written inside the tag.

---

## 8. What Is an Event?

An event is something that happens on the page, such as a click, a key press, or a mouse movement.

JavaScript can wait for an event and react only when it occurs.

---

## 9. Listening for a Click with `addEventListener()`

```javascript
sanctuaryLink.addEventListener("click", function () {
  console.log("You clicked the Sanctuary link!");
});
```

`.addEventListener()` attaches a listener to an element.

`"click"` names the event being watched for.

The function inside the parentheses has no name of its own. It is called an anonymous function, and the browser runs it automatically whenever the event occurs.

This code produces no output when the page loads. It only runs after the click happens.

---

## 10. Adding a Listener to Every Chamber

A loop can attach the same listener to every element in a list.

```javascript
for (const link of allLinks) {
  link.addEventListener("click", function () {
    console.log(link.textContent + " was clicked.");
  });
}
```

All six chambers now listen for a click, using one block of code instead of six.

---

## 11. Reading and Changing Classes with `classList`

`.classList` provides access to an element's CSS classes.

```javascript
otherLink.classList.remove("active");
link.classList.add("active");
```

`.remove("active")` takes the `active` class off an element.

`.add("active")` places the `active` class onto an element.

Removing or adding a class changes how `garden.css` styles that element immediately.

---

## 12. Assembling the Navigation Feature

```javascript
for (const link of allLinks) {
  link.addEventListener("click", function () {
    for (const otherLink of allLinks) {
      otherLink.classList.remove("active");
    }
    link.classList.add("active");
  });
}
```

The outer loop attaches a click listener to all six chambers.

Inside each listener, an inner loop removes `active` from every chamber, without exception.

Once every chamber has been cleared, `active` is added back onto only the one that was clicked.

Clearing every chamber first, then setting the one exception, is simpler and more reliable than trying to track which single chamber was previously active.

---

## 13. A Simple Model of This Chapter

```text
querySelectorAll finds every chamber
          ↓
for...of visits each chamber in turn
          ↓
addEventListener waits for a click on each one
          ↓
On click, every chamber loses the active class
          ↓
The clicked chamber receives the active class back
```

---

## 🏰 Example From Nurgle's Garden

```javascript
const allLinks = document.querySelectorAll(".navigation-link");

for (const link of allLinks) {
  link.addEventListener("click", function () {
    for (const otherLink of allLinks) {
      otherLink.classList.remove("active");
    }
    link.classList.add("active");
  });
}
```

Clicking the Forge moved the active styling away from the Sanctuary and onto the Forge.

Clicking the Archives moved it again.

No chamber ever held the active styling twice at once.

---

## ⚠️ Common Mistakes

### Mistake 1: Expecting `querySelector()` to return more than one element

```javascript
const link = document.querySelector(".navigation-link");
```

This returns only the first match.

Use `querySelectorAll()` to reach every match.

---

### Mistake 2: Forgetting that lists start at `0`

```text
allLinks[1]
```

This refers to the second chamber, not the first.

---

### Mistake 3: Expecting a listener to run immediately

```javascript
sanctuaryLink.addEventListener("click", function () {
  console.log("Clicked");
});
```

This produces no output until an actual click occurs.

---

### Mistake 4: Clearing the active class after adding it

```javascript
link.classList.add("active");
for (const otherLink of allLinks) {
  otherLink.classList.remove("active");
}
```

This order removes `active` from the very chamber that just received it.

Clear every chamber first. Add the class back afterward.

---

### Mistake 5: Confusing `console.log()` with a change to the page

`console.log()` writes only to the Console.

Only DOM methods such as `classList` change what a visitor actually sees.

---

## ✅ Best Practices

- Use `querySelectorAll()` when more than one element needs to be reached.
- Remember that list positions begin at `0`.
- Attach listeners inside a loop when the same behavior applies to every item in a list.
- Clear a shared state before setting the one exception.
- Confirm a DOM change on the actual page, not only in the Console.

